    "problem_statement": "# HTML output in Jupyter notebooks is displayed in uppercase\n\n## Describe the bug\nWhen using PrettyTable in a Jupyter notebook, the HTML output is displayed in all uppercase letters. This makes the tables difficult to read and doesn't match the expected behavior.\n\n## How to Reproduce\n1. Create a PrettyTable in a Jupyter notebook\n2. Display the table\n\n```python\nfrom prettytable import PrettyTable\n\n# Create a table\ntable = PrettyTable()\ntable.field_names = [\"Name\", \"Age\", \"City\"]\ntable.add_row([\"Alice\", 24, \"New York\"])\ntable.add_row([\"Bob\", 32, \"Los Angeles\"])\ntable.add_row([\"Charlie\", 18, \"Chicago\"])\n\n# Display the table in a Jupyter notebook cell\ntable\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\nThe table should be displayed with normal case text, matching the HTML string that would be generated by `get_html_string()`.\n\n## Actual behavior\nThe table is displayed with ALL UPPERCASE text, making it difficult to read and not matching the expected styling.\n\n## Screenshots\n![Screenshot showing uppercase HTML table output in Jupyter](https://example.com/screenshot.png)\n\n## Environment\n- OS: Ubuntu 20.04\n- Python version: 3.10\n- PrettyTable version: 3.5.0\n- Jupyter: notebook 6.4.12\n\n## Additional context\nThis only happens when displaying tables directly in Jupyter notebooks. When using `print(table)` or viewing the HTML output in other contexts, the case is preserved correctly."
  },
